  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 338 AM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\n- Sunday Morning Fog\n\nAn overnight flow of near-surface moisture off Lake Erie is \nresulting in another morning of dense fog, this time mainly east \nof US-127, with patchy/shallow fog possible to the west. The REFS \nhas been handing fog probabilities fairly well the past few \nnights. Fog potential Sunday night is lower given cloudier skies.\n\n- Cooler Monday-Tuesday, Then Warming Again\n\nA cold front sagging through on Monday will knock temperatures \ndown to closer to normal for Monday and Tuesday. Roughly, highs \nare favored to be in the mid 50s in Central MI to mid 60s in \nSouthern MI. This front is likely to be dry, although there could \nbe light rain showers or sprinkles in Central MI Sunday night to \nMonday morning.\n\nThere is good agreement among the ensembles for temperatures to \nrebound Wednesday, back well into the 60s to around 70, as low-\nlevel southwesterly flow on the backside of an eastern CONUS high \ntransports 10C 850 mb air overhead. There is the also good \npotential for this relative warmth to persist through the end of \nthe week/month, though a minority of members (20 percent) cool off\nhighs into the 50s on Friday/Saturday with a deeper eastern \nCanadian trough or weaker southeast CONUS ridge.\n\n- Chances for Showers Late Week\n\nAbout a 20-30 percent chance for rain showers amounting to less \nthan a quarter inch returns between late Wednesday and late \nThursday, with increasing midlevel moisture and a hint of \ninstability ahead of a weakening cold front. Still too soon to say\nwhether rain will affect Halloween or hold off until after, \nthough PoPs are presently around 40 percent for Saturday evening. \nThere is a fairly large constituency of ensemble member solutions \nthat brings a soaking rainfall (around 0.5 to 2 inches) in early \nNovember, as the southern jet stream becomes more active across \nmuch of the CONUS.",
